{-
   What a generic *call* refuses - milestone 6, waypoints G1 and G2.

   # resolver: ownership

   Separate from `Reject.Declaration.yana` because of the resolver's phase order: every declaration
   is resolved before any body is, and a file whose declarations failed has no bodies walked - so one
   file holding both groups could only ever report the first.
-}

fn id(->x: a) -> a = x

data Boxed(a) {value: a}

-- A variable the *enclosing* signature decides, reached through a field - and not a rejection.
--
-- A call whose result decides its type argument resolves - `used(blank())` picks `blank`'s `a` off
-- the parameter it is handed to. This used to be refused on the argument that the field of a
-- construction whose own argument is still open supplies no expectation, "because a hint is what a
-- literal defaults to as well and a variable is not something a literal can be". A literal *is*
-- something a variable can be now - it is `FromInt(a)`'s call - so a position whose type is the
-- enclosing signature's own variable is a decided position (`mentionsContext`), and `nothing()`
-- here is typed at that `a`. Kept beside the rejections as the witness that it resolves.
fn empty() -> Boxed(a) = Boxed {value: nothing()}

fn nothing() -> a = nothing()

-- A record parameter no field mentions, which is the same question asked of a constructor.
data Phantom(a) {count: I32}

fn phantom() -> I32:
    let p = Phantom {count: 1}
    return p.count

-- Two arguments deciding one variable differently. The binding is deliberately *not* what reports:
-- the first argument makes the second's expected type `I32`, and the position then reports the way
-- it reports every other value that does not fit - which names both sides, where a message about an
-- inference step disagreeing with itself would name neither.
fn same(->x: a, ->y: a) -> a = x

fn mismatched() -> I32 = same(1, True)

{-
   A field requirement's result decided twice, differently.

   `a.mark: b` decides `b` from the subject, which is what makes a generic field usable at all - but
   a call that also *supplies* `b` from an argument has two answers to a question the dependency
   promised has one. Reported against the field rather than against the inference step, because what
   the author has to fix is one of the two types they wrote.
-}
data Marked {mark: I32}

fn (a.mark: b) twiceDecided(x: a, other: b) -> I32 = 0

fn disagreeing() -> I32 = twiceDecided(Marked {mark: 1 :: I32}, 2 :: I64)

fn main() -> I32 = id(0)
